  "result": "Perfect! All 114 tests pass. Let me verify the complete implementation:\n\n**Summary of VAT implementation:**\n\n**Updated `src/invoices.js`:**\n- **`invoiceTotals()`** now returns `{ subtotal, discount, vat, total }` where:\n  - `subtotal` = sum of all line totals (qty \u00d7 unitPrice)\n  - `discount` = sum of rounded line discounts\n  - `vat` = sum of rounded per-line VATs (each line's VAT = roundCents(lineAmountAfterDiscount \u00d7 vatRate / 100))\n  - `total` = subtotal - discount + vat (clamped at 0)\n\n**Updated `src/render.js`:**\n- Shows VAT line `VAT: +\u20acX.XX` just above the total (only if VAT > 0)\n- Uses `formatEuros()` for all monetary amounts\n\n**Test coverage (19 new VAT-related tests):**\n- VAT calculated on line amount after discount\n- VAT rates: 0%, 9%, 21%\n- Mixed VAT rates on same invoice\n- VAT rounded per line (banker's rounding)\n- VAT with discounts\n- VAT-exempt lines\n- Total clamping with VAT\n- Rendering with VAT display\n- Rendering without VAT (VAT-exempt products)\n- Integration with tier discounts and line discounts\n\nAll calculations follow the decision that **VAT is computed per line on the line's amount after discount and rounded per line; the invoice's VAT is the sum of the rounded line VATs, never a percentage of the invoice total.**\n\nAll 114 tests pass, including proper banker's rounding (half to even) for all VAT calculations.",
